In C, you can terminate a program by using the exit function. The exit function causes the program to terminate immediately.



```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
  printf("Hello, world!\n");
  exit(0);
}
```
The exit function takes one argument, which is the status code. The status code is a number that indicates the result of the program. A status code of 0 means that the program ran successfully. Any other value indicates that something went wrong.
You can also use the exit function to terminate a program with a status code other than 0. For example, the following program terminates with a status code of 1:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
  printf("Hello, world!\n");
  exit(1);
}
```
